1
00:00:01,528 --> 00:00:05,291
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to ask you for advice on how to lead a secular Buddhist group.

2
00:00:05,851 --> 00:00:11,034
[SPEAKER_00]: People are coming to our group expecting meditation to relax them.

3
00:00:11,094 --> 00:00:13,255
[SPEAKER_00]: We don't read suttas or think about them.

4
00:00:13,576 --> 00:00:17,598
[SPEAKER_00]: New people who are coming most probably are not keeping precepts.

5
00:00:17,638 --> 00:00:23,022
[SPEAKER_00]: So what should we do so that people can benefit from correct Buddhist practice?

6
00:00:23,182 --> 00:00:24,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you just answer it yourself.

7
00:00:26,123 --> 00:00:28,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Read the suttas and teach them to keep the precepts.

8
00:00:29,395 --> 00:00:32,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you might as well not call it the Theravāda Buddhist group.

9
00:00:34,637 --> 00:00:35,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I agree.

10
00:00:36,437 --> 00:00:44,080
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is a big conviction that people don't want to read suttas because it's boring for them.

11
00:00:44,580 --> 00:00:47,341
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, then basically they would go to another group.

12
00:00:48,762 --> 00:00:56,284
[SPEAKER_01]: But if they come to learn about the Buddhist teaching, you present them the Buddhist teaching.

13
00:00:56,324 --> 00:00:58,205
[SPEAKER_00]: If they don't like it, that's not your problem.

14
00:00:59,761 --> 00:01:03,968
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like... your goal is not to try and keep as many people as possible interested.

15
00:01:04,008 --> 00:01:07,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Your goal is to show to those they want to follow it, what to follow.

16
00:01:12,748 --> 00:01:21,833
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and what to do with this expectation that meditation is some kind of relaxation technique?

17
00:01:22,093 --> 00:01:26,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, as I said, like people, everybody that starts, they don't really, they can't tell the difference.

18
00:01:26,995 --> 00:01:30,717
[SPEAKER_01]: So, everybody will have their own expectations as putujanas that they come from.

19
00:01:31,017 --> 00:01:31,237
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

20
00:01:32,167 --> 00:01:34,708
[SPEAKER_01]: What you do is, you provide them the right information.

21
00:01:34,989 --> 00:01:39,951
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if they don't like it, they can find something else that suits their preferences more.

22
00:01:40,532 --> 00:01:49,136
[SPEAKER_01]: But you'll be surprised how, despite of how wrong their expectation might be, if people were to hear the right thing, many of them would recognize that they want to do it.

23
00:01:50,797 --> 00:01:55,600
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they come with an expectation, but you provide them with the actual teachings, so the suttas.

24
00:01:56,995 --> 00:02:03,037
[SPEAKER_01]: The precepts, the training in those things that we often talk about, and those that see the value in that, they will stay.

25
00:02:03,418 --> 00:02:07,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Those that don't, will find something else that would cater to their needs.

26
00:02:08,199 --> 00:02:08,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

27
00:02:09,720 --> 00:02:18,883
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I have a theory that everyone deep inside knows what is the truth, but we just choose to not see it.

28
00:02:19,103 --> 00:02:19,584
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

29
00:02:19,624 --> 00:02:20,424
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't make them.

30
00:02:21,779 --> 00:02:29,305
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if they don't want to practice the Buddha's teaching once they find out what it is, that's up to them and that's fine.

31
00:02:30,826 --> 00:02:36,071
[SPEAKER_01]: But the worst is to actually misrepresent the Buddha's teaching because you think people might not like it.

32
00:02:36,231 --> 00:02:38,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, there's always somebody who's not going to like something.

33
00:02:38,993 --> 00:02:47,319
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you want to run a group, your duty is to present it as clearly as possible what the Buddha was teaching, what the requirements are.

34
00:02:48,881 --> 00:02:50,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if that puts everybody away.

35
00:02:52,499 --> 00:02:54,982
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you recommend some suttas for starters?

36
00:02:56,304 --> 00:03:00,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I think just you could just go generally just go through Majjhima Nikāya suttas.

37
00:03:04,329 --> 00:03:06,451
[SPEAKER_01]: They kind of have a certain, like, balance.

38
00:03:06,471 --> 00:03:10,994
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, they're not too short, they're not too long, and they always, obviously, revolve around the important points.

39
00:03:11,114 --> 00:03:31,531
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just any, pretty much, engagement with the suttas can sort of point people in the right direction, so at least they'll know, okay, you know, I can do this on my own as well, I can read this, I can study this, I can try and understand what these texts kind of require from you to do.

40
00:03:34,126 --> 00:03:36,307
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're interested in following the Buddha's teaching, yeah.

41
00:03:38,568 --> 00:03:41,249
[SPEAKER_00]: Should we advise them to do some contemplation?

42
00:03:41,289 --> 00:03:44,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, for example, ask yourself, are you suffering?

43
00:03:44,811 --> 00:03:45,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Why are you suffering?

44
00:03:46,051 --> 00:03:47,452
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, all of that, of course, yeah.

45
00:03:48,212 --> 00:03:56,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, kind of, a certain, like, a guided type of questioning, reflections and so on.

46
00:03:57,036 --> 00:03:58,077
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said,

47
00:03:59,911 --> 00:04:03,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Your job is to present them with what the Buddha was teaching.

48
00:04:04,176 --> 00:04:09,481
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might see that, oh, half of these people here come because they think the Buddha was teaching something completely different.

49
00:04:10,382 --> 00:04:12,344
[SPEAKER_01]: So, therefore, I won't tell them what the Buddha was... No.

50
00:04:12,504 --> 00:04:14,887
[SPEAKER_01]: You tell them, and if that makes them leave, they leave.

51
00:04:15,007 --> 00:04:15,828
[SPEAKER_01]: New people will come.

52
00:04:21,124 --> 00:04:43,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and I guess, well, we are not gonna, eh, just remove the meditation, because it's, it's used, use it so, it is used so often, and people expect expectations... Yeah, you don't have to remove the period of sitting quietly, but you certainly remove the wrong instructions of meditation.

53
00:04:44,749 --> 00:04:52,195
[SPEAKER_01]: That are simply not found in the suttas and come with the commentaries and contemporary kind of Theravāda watering down and perversion of the teaching.

54
00:04:53,096 --> 00:05:06,086
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just, yeah, go through a sutta, do some questioning, some basic kind of reflection on certain themes that are from the suttas, encourage people to think about it, and then tell them, sit quietly now with those reflections.

55
00:05:07,770 --> 00:05:12,333
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's assume I am a new person who comes to your secular Buddhist group.

56
00:05:13,394 --> 00:05:17,697
[SPEAKER_00]: I have an expectation that I want to just relax, to feel better.

57
00:05:17,897 --> 00:05:19,478
[SPEAKER_00]: I came here to relax.

58
00:05:20,419 --> 00:05:21,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you tell them you came to the wrong place.

59
00:05:22,340 --> 00:05:24,622
[SPEAKER_01]: This is not a relaxation center.

60
00:05:24,642 --> 00:05:26,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Go to a spa center if you want to relax.

61
00:05:27,504 --> 00:05:34,990
[SPEAKER_01]: In this place, we learn what the Buddha was teaching, which is suffering and freedom from it, which is not going to be a relaxing truth for most people when they first hear it.

62
00:05:37,917 --> 00:05:46,563
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have no duty or obligation to cater to the expectations of people when they come for the Buddha's teaching.

63
00:05:47,624 --> 00:05:48,604
[SPEAKER_01]: Supposedly, at least.

64
00:05:49,245 --> 00:05:49,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

65
00:05:49,605 --> 00:05:50,966
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you told me this.

66
00:05:51,266 --> 00:05:53,648
[SPEAKER_00]: I decided still not to leave.

67
00:05:54,228 --> 00:05:55,369
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what's next?

68
00:05:55,509 --> 00:05:56,089
[SPEAKER_00]: I am...

69
00:05:56,269 --> 00:05:57,951
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to relax because I am suffering.

70
00:05:58,391 --> 00:05:58,591
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

71
00:05:58,651 --> 00:06:01,533
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way to truly relax from your suffering is to understand it.

72
00:06:01,713 --> 00:06:04,435
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to start study the suttas, training the precepts,

73
00:06:06,771 --> 00:06:08,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Until you relax on the right level.

74
00:06:08,712 --> 00:06:08,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

75
00:06:10,053 --> 00:06:12,395
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want a quick fix, you came to a wrong place.

76
00:06:16,077 --> 00:06:27,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, and often I got question, we got question, when some new people come, where is Buddhism a religion?

77
00:06:27,384 --> 00:06:28,665
[SPEAKER_01]: It depends how you look at it, I suppose.

78
00:06:30,206 --> 00:06:32,067
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's certainly today is a religion.

79
00:06:33,235 --> 00:06:48,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when you look at the Buddhism as the way it was spread through various Asian countries, it is their religion, it's on the level of their belief, on the level of their superstition, just like how Christianity is in Europe for most people.

80
00:06:49,361 --> 00:06:54,743
[SPEAKER_01]: But the practicing aspect of Buddhism, or the practicing aspect of any religion, it's not really religion.

81
00:06:56,228 --> 00:07:01,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Religion is basically, when it becomes kind of a part of identity and belief of people, they don't really practice.

82
00:07:02,071 --> 00:07:04,352
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't want to practice.

83
00:07:04,372 --> 00:07:07,594
[SPEAKER_00]: They're satisfied with that external, just as I said, identifier.

84
00:07:08,755 --> 00:07:18,140
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in terms of its customs, rules, rituals and stuff, yeah, of course, it's very religious, like everything else that has reached that level of institution.

85
00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:19,701
[SPEAKER_01]: But is the dhamma religion?

86
00:07:19,881 --> 00:07:20,781
[SPEAKER_01]: No, of course not.

87
00:07:22,122 --> 00:07:23,743
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not even a philosophy for that matter.

88
00:07:25,692 --> 00:07:34,819
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a specific recipe for a certain type of behavior, certain types of reflections, that can bring certain type of understanding.

89
00:07:35,299 --> 00:07:35,620
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

90
00:07:36,280 --> 00:07:37,381
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's very specific.

91
00:07:42,605 --> 00:07:51,493
[SPEAKER_00]: When people ask, like, how long will it take to make some progress, visible progress, in dhamma, what should I answer?

92
00:07:51,613 --> 00:07:54,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it depends how long do they make a good quality effort.

93
00:07:56,250 --> 00:08:02,833
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same, somebody goes to become a carpenter, hasn't even started yet, but wants to know how long it will take me to become a master expert.

94
00:08:02,893 --> 00:08:04,054
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's up to you.

95
00:08:05,134 --> 00:08:08,476
[SPEAKER_01]: If you do it rightly from the start, it will take you very little time.

96
00:08:08,616 --> 00:08:10,177
[SPEAKER_01]: If not, so...

97
00:08:10,697 --> 00:08:14,198
[SPEAKER_01]: If that's what's important to that person, how long will it take me to reach that?

98
00:08:14,959 --> 00:08:19,621
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, then it should be equally important to them to start doing it rightly from the beginning and not delay the work.

99
00:08:26,057 --> 00:08:31,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and what should I tell people?

100
00:08:31,379 --> 00:08:40,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, we are advising people to watch your videos, but some people are saying, like, you are too harsh in speech.

101
00:08:40,623 --> 00:08:42,644
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you are not positive enough.

102
00:08:43,264 --> 00:08:47,426
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you don't have this image of a smiley monk.

103
00:08:47,986 --> 00:08:52,948
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and they are... it is too much for them.

104
00:08:53,169 --> 00:08:54,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it might be too much.

105
00:08:57,185 --> 00:09:00,186
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to work your way up to be able to understand the Buddha's teaching.

106
00:09:03,266 --> 00:09:11,348
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, like those, you know, there are those instances in the suttas when that man went to the Buddha and said that his son died or something.

107
00:09:12,268 --> 00:09:18,890
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, he was saying how children are the greatest happiness and the greatest wealth and the greatest benefit.

108
00:09:19,490 --> 00:09:24,431
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said, no, children are the greatest suffering, the source of the biggest suffering.

109
00:09:25,631 --> 00:09:36,620
[SPEAKER_01]: And he disagreed, he dismissed, he said something, and then went to these random gamblers and drunkards to ask them the same question, because he knew they will agree with him.

110
00:09:38,601 --> 00:09:41,163
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if people are not willing to question their own preferences,

111
00:09:42,150 --> 00:09:55,081
[SPEAKER_01]: of what the practice is, while at the same time knowing that they don't know what the practice is, but still use their own preferences as a criteria of, oh, I like this, I don't like that, then there is no dhamma for them.

112
00:09:55,362 --> 00:09:58,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, they're all... they're not able to even begin thinking rightly.

113
00:10:00,606 --> 00:10:09,474
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if somebody expects the dhamma to be a smiley, laughing monk, just talking about happiness and positive thoughts, I mean, there's nothing wrong to expect that.

114
00:10:10,572 --> 00:10:15,995
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, if you meet somebody that tells you the opposite, do you just dismiss it on account of a mere preference?

115
00:10:16,475 --> 00:10:20,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he doesn't look right or doesn't play the right part that I expect him to play?

116
00:10:21,030 --> 00:10:23,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Or do you question your own preferences and beliefs?

117
00:10:23,351 --> 00:10:25,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Because what position are you in?

118
00:10:25,872 --> 00:10:27,753
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you the one seeking the way out of suffering?

119
00:10:27,793 --> 00:10:34,855
[SPEAKER_01]: In which case, if you genuinely think your head is on fire, you would certainly be considering all options that are presented to you.

120
00:10:34,895 --> 00:10:38,637
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be dismissing them because you have... you think you have a privilege to do so.

121
00:10:38,657 --> 00:10:39,377
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I don't like this.

122
00:10:39,517 --> 00:10:40,497
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to listen to it.

123
00:10:42,318 --> 00:10:44,839
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, they're not serious enough to practice to begin with.

124
00:10:44,859 --> 00:10:50,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if they were, they would think about things in both ways, regardless of their preferences.

125
00:10:53,066 --> 00:10:57,428
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if they want a happy monk and positive thoughts, they don't want the dhamma, really.

126
00:10:58,409 --> 00:11:07,253
[SPEAKER_01]: They just want an engaging, even intoxicating, spiritual dimension to their very kind of household, worldly life.

127
00:11:07,273 --> 00:11:08,253
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't really want a change.

128
00:11:10,394 --> 00:11:17,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there a place for such people who really don't want dhamma, but they want just kind of Buddhist practice for lay people?

129
00:11:18,178 --> 00:11:18,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Say again?

130
00:11:18,638 --> 00:11:19,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there what?

131
00:11:19,899 --> 00:11:21,140
[SPEAKER_01]: A place for such people.

132
00:11:21,400 --> 00:11:21,880
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you mean?

133
00:11:22,622 --> 00:11:23,783
[SPEAKER_00]: In our group, for example.

134
00:11:25,963 --> 00:11:28,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it depends what you want your group to be about.

135
00:11:29,564 --> 00:11:38,427
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you want your group to be about something that just caters to your average Buddhist, with whatever preconceived notions of Buddhism they bring in?

136
00:11:38,447 --> 00:11:39,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you want to just cater to that?

137
00:11:40,187 --> 00:11:44,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Or do you want your group to be about the actual Buddhist teaching?

138
00:11:45,289 --> 00:11:48,750
[SPEAKER_01]: In which case, there is no place for those that don't want to follow the actual Buddhist teaching.

139
00:11:50,896 --> 00:11:54,679
[SPEAKER_00]: But maybe they just want these teachings for lay people from Buddha.

140
00:11:55,159 --> 00:11:58,802
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same teaching.

141
00:12:00,403 --> 00:12:02,384
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't teach a different dhamma to lay people.

142
00:12:03,285 --> 00:12:09,309
[SPEAKER_01]: And when he was teaching lay people how to look after their wives and be generous and good householder, that was not the dhamma.

143
00:12:09,349 --> 00:12:11,970
[SPEAKER_01]: That was just be a good person so you have a good rebirth.

144
00:12:12,531 --> 00:12:15,793
[SPEAKER_01]: And he would teach them that because he knew they won't understand the dhamma.

145
00:12:17,147 --> 00:12:21,689
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your definition of dhamma is things that uproot the suffering.

146
00:12:21,769 --> 00:12:22,049
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course.

147
00:12:22,349 --> 00:12:24,269
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the only definition there is.

148
00:12:25,030 --> 00:12:30,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Because many people have definition like all teachings are dhamma, everything in the world is dhamma.

149
00:12:30,591 --> 00:12:32,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because they haven't read the suttas and haven't read the actual dhamma.

150
00:12:34,113 --> 00:12:39,476
[SPEAKER_01]: They've just been hearing second-hand perverted accounts and developed their own notions of who knows what, basically.

151
00:12:39,516 --> 00:12:44,299
[SPEAKER_01]: Some generic religious spirituality, Advaita, and so on.

152
00:12:44,659 --> 00:12:52,123
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as the Buddha said repeatedly, dhamma only has one taste, which is freedom from suffering.

153
00:12:52,143 --> 00:12:56,425
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, the ocean has only one taste of salt.

154
00:12:57,066 --> 00:12:59,047
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether he was teaching it to householders,

155
00:12:59,975 --> 00:13:12,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Or lay... or monks, if it was the dhamma being taught, the principles, the gradual, the virtue, the restraint, the taming of the mind, reflections on the certain... certain topics, there was no exception.

156
00:13:12,979 --> 00:13:13,439
[SPEAKER_01]: It was the same.

157
00:13:13,459 --> 00:13:17,400
[SPEAKER_01]: And there was... as soon as they say that, whether householder or a monk, this is the... this is the path.

158
00:13:19,777 --> 00:13:24,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so if people come there and they don't want to do that, then there is no place in that group for them.

159
00:13:25,178 --> 00:13:27,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because you refuse to let them in.

160
00:13:27,299 --> 00:13:30,379
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because they refuse to follow what's required.

161
00:13:32,660 --> 00:13:36,780
[SPEAKER_01]: It's again like you go to a carpentry school and you say, but I don't want to work with the wood.

162
00:13:38,501 --> 00:13:40,241
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't like that, but I want to be a carpenter.

163
00:13:40,561 --> 00:13:43,022
[SPEAKER_01]: I still want to be a carpenter, but I don't want to work with wood.

164
00:13:44,042 --> 00:13:45,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Does that make any sense?

165
00:13:45,322 --> 00:13:45,482
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

166
00:13:46,322 --> 00:13:49,163
[SPEAKER_01]: By any measure of rational thought, that's insane.

167
00:13:50,416 --> 00:13:53,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Especially when you find out what carpentry entails.

168
00:13:54,578 --> 00:13:56,618
[SPEAKER_00]: If you still want to insist on your own preferences.

169
00:14:01,300 --> 00:14:04,481
[SPEAKER_00]: Should we also include some kind of practice of generosity?

170
00:14:05,422 --> 00:14:05,722
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

171
00:14:06,842 --> 00:14:07,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

172
00:14:08,903 --> 00:14:11,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Any advice what it might be?

173
00:14:12,163 --> 00:14:25,693
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I don't really know, but you could probably find out, you know, places, communities, things like that, that are in need of help, that can be contributed towards and just organize things like that.

174
00:14:25,913 --> 00:14:26,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

175
00:14:29,615 --> 00:14:32,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Like go and volunteer somewhere as a group, basically.

176
00:14:32,217 --> 00:14:32,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

177
00:14:33,798 --> 00:14:34,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, absolutely.

178
00:14:38,623 --> 00:14:51,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so many teachers, eh, you are focusing on five precepts, but many teachers, eh, put emphasis on Eightfold Path and other parts of path as well.

179
00:14:52,287 --> 00:14:52,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Mm-hmm.

180
00:14:53,567 --> 00:15:02,669
[SPEAKER_01]: And so... Well, those teachers should read the suttas, because, eh, they very clearly say that there is no Eightfold Path without the right view.

181
00:15:04,270 --> 00:15:08,131
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you think you're practicing Eightfold Path without being a sotāpanna, you're not.

182
00:15:09,279 --> 00:15:09,879
[SPEAKER_01]: As simple as that.

183
00:15:10,380 --> 00:15:11,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Right view comes first.

184
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:15,782
[SPEAKER_01]: All the individual factors of the Eightfold Path arise from the right view.

185
00:15:16,923 --> 00:15:19,444
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, the Sutta cannot be any more clear on that.

186
00:15:20,845 --> 00:15:24,767
[SPEAKER_01]: So I would, I would advise them to basically read the Suttas.

187
00:15:24,807 --> 00:15:27,468
[SPEAKER_00]: How about Myungdang, right view?

188
00:15:28,910 --> 00:15:29,430
[SPEAKER_01]: What about it?

189
00:15:30,471 --> 00:15:39,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is a split for mundane right view and... Well, in a mundane right view, again, there is no ennoblative path in it.

190
00:15:40,354 --> 00:15:46,477
[SPEAKER_01]: The mundane right view is just the recognition, pretty much recognizing the weight of your actions.

191
00:15:47,852 --> 00:16:05,225
[SPEAKER_01]: that you are inseparable from them, that there are things that basically your views will not necessarily comprehend there and then, but that's why you also recognize the possibility of existence of such things, so you don't dismiss them.

192
00:16:06,853 --> 00:16:14,819
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, the mundane right view is just putujana, who is kind of more acutely and more transparently, more authentically aware of his own situation.

193
00:16:15,299 --> 00:16:21,243
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's described like there is mother, there is father, there is good action, there is bad action, there are results of action.

194
00:16:21,623 --> 00:16:29,769
[SPEAKER_01]: Because even those basic things today, if you were to press people to ask them for it, they would kind of fall into these views of denial or a cover-up and so on.

195
00:16:30,600 --> 00:16:40,746
[SPEAKER_01]: And everything would just be boiled down to certain, I don't know, like reductionist psychology, or biology, or physiology, and in reality nothing's real, everything is constantly changing.

196
00:16:41,166 --> 00:16:42,627
[SPEAKER_01]: All of those are just forms of denial.

197
00:16:43,607 --> 00:16:52,813
[SPEAKER_01]: So even most Buddhists today, that follow this commentarial level of teaching, don't even have the mundane right view of recognizing, no, there is good action and bad action.

198
00:16:53,665 --> 00:16:58,449
[SPEAKER_01]: There is good thought and bad thought, and the weight of those things are on me, for myself.

199
00:16:59,049 --> 00:17:02,091
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no shared world here that we all partake together.

200
00:17:02,992 --> 00:17:04,033
[SPEAKER_01]: It's me and everybody else.

201
00:17:04,273 --> 00:17:05,153
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how things work.

202
00:17:05,374 --> 00:17:13,920
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I am the one bound with my own actions, heir to them, and gonna inherit everything implicit, to the extent that I cannot even fathom.

203
00:17:16,415 --> 00:17:20,317
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just, yeah, I can measure the action and the reaction that comes with it.

204
00:17:20,337 --> 00:17:20,478
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

205
00:17:20,938 --> 00:17:25,581
[SPEAKER_01]: The implications of your actions can span over a course of who knows how many lifetimes.

206
00:17:26,021 --> 00:17:27,682
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't even fathom that at a time.

207
00:17:28,422 --> 00:17:32,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, they will keep coming... the results will keep coming back.

208
00:17:34,466 --> 00:17:40,670
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the fact that there are those things that you cannot even comprehend, does not give you the rational basis to dismiss them.

209
00:17:41,637 --> 00:17:45,460
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, like, usually, like, people say, oh, what's the proof that there is rebirth?

210
00:17:45,580 --> 00:17:47,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what's the proof that there isn't one?

211
00:17:49,184 --> 00:17:58,411
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have no proof for either, yet the lack of proof for rebirth immediately justifies the vast majority of people in their belief that there is no rebirth, because there is no proof for it.

212
00:17:59,092 --> 00:18:00,894
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, but there is no proof against it either.

213
00:18:01,734 --> 00:18:03,276
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why did you take up that belief?

214
00:18:04,176 --> 00:18:08,961
[SPEAKER_01]: And you would say, oh, because you were not transparent and clear enough that you are in a position of not knowing either,

215
00:18:09,915 --> 00:18:16,682
[SPEAKER_01]: And therefore, falling into a belief about one or the other, will basically just be a way of a compromise and a cover-up.

216
00:18:22,208 --> 00:18:31,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's say that some new people are coming, and should we tell them that there is a river, and then they should contemplate.

217
00:18:40,655 --> 00:18:50,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yes, again, like, in a broad strokes, but that will not necessarily develop the mundane right view either for them.

218
00:18:50,823 --> 00:18:59,410
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it needs to come internally, a person themselves, internally, need to see that, oh, I'm in a position of not knowing this for sure.

219
00:19:00,956 --> 00:19:06,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, whatever I choose to believe, will in return reflect on my course of action.

220
00:19:07,439 --> 00:19:24,949
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why when they asked the Buddha the same question, he said basically to recognize that, well, I don't know which one's true, but usually, if I'm dismissing the rebirth, if I'm dismissing the implications of future kind of results of my action, I won't pay much attention to what I do in this life.

221
00:19:25,914 --> 00:19:26,635
[SPEAKER_01]: I will be careless.

222
00:19:26,655 --> 00:19:28,536
[SPEAKER_01]: I will be acting out of greed, aversion, delusion.

223
00:19:29,337 --> 00:19:44,369
[SPEAKER_01]: If I kind of lean on the side, poof, there could be a rebirth, which means then there will be implications and results of my actions to the a lot greater degree than I can measure in this life, then you would pay attention to what you do, what you say, what you do to others and how you behave.

224
00:19:46,591 --> 00:19:50,775
[SPEAKER_01]: But it needs to come from your own, as I said, internal recognition and set of values.

225
00:19:51,475 --> 00:19:53,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just, I'm a Buddhist, I believe in rebirth.

226
00:19:54,700 --> 00:19:58,762
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, well, that did not make you any more transparent than before.

227
00:19:59,602 --> 00:20:05,024
[SPEAKER_01]: You just took another external stance in regard to your position, because you refused to endure that ambiguity of your situation.

228
00:20:08,906 --> 00:20:14,288
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's say that some new people are coming and they are not keeping precepts.

229
00:20:14,428 --> 00:20:19,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Should we advise them doing contemplation of anatta, sickness, death?

230
00:20:21,331 --> 00:20:21,691
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean...

231
00:20:22,907 --> 00:20:38,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, in broad strokes, you could point out that those are the contemplations that one needs to develop, but then you also need to very specifically tell them that contemplating such things without a basis of virtue and certain degree of being able to contain your own mind will not end well.

232
00:20:39,357 --> 00:20:41,359
[SPEAKER_01]: It has a risk, carries a risk of not ending well.

233
00:20:42,780 --> 00:20:50,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you are prodding, you're provoking your mind now towards the contexts and contemplations that naturally no mind wants to think.

234
00:20:51,842 --> 00:20:54,903
[SPEAKER_01]: But at the same time, you're keeping it wild to do whatever he wants.

235
00:20:56,644 --> 00:21:03,486
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you agitate it and then you make it... and then you allow it to behave whichever way he wants as a result of that agitation.

236
00:21:03,546 --> 00:21:05,606
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's not going to be good.

237
00:21:06,807 --> 00:21:12,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it safe... Is it a safe contemplation for people who are not keeping precepts?

238
00:21:12,789 --> 00:21:15,290
[SPEAKER_01]: The value of keeping the precepts and the danger of not keeping them.

239
00:21:15,470 --> 00:21:16,890
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a safe contemplation for them.

240
00:21:18,911 --> 00:21:19,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, makes sense.

241
00:21:23,249 --> 00:21:27,876
[SPEAKER_00]: So we can just talk about white-keeping precepts.

242
00:21:28,216 --> 00:21:28,857
[SPEAKER_00]: It's good for you.

243
00:21:29,578 --> 00:21:32,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and why not keeping them is dangerous.

244
00:21:34,004 --> 00:21:37,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, not because somebody will say, oh, you're not a good Buddhist.

245
00:21:38,059 --> 00:21:54,150
[SPEAKER_01]: But because it keeps the very core of your being untamed, wild, whimsical, subject to extreme fluctuations of moods and exposures on the emotional level, and you're going to suffer as a result of it.

